import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.DYPTBNgj.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Examples","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/examples.md","filePath":"guide/examples.md"}'),n={name:"guide/examples.md"};function s(i,e,h,l,d,g){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="examples" tabindex="-1">Examples <a class="header-anchor" href="#examples" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Examples&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Seven worked examples, each a plain-language walkthrough of a complete workflow: what it does, which nodes it uses, and how to wire them together. None of these are one-click imports from this page. For four of the seven, building one means opening a new workflow in Aerini and following its steps by hand on the canvas; Getting Started ships differently, as a Docker package, and the last two also come as workflow files you can drag onto the canvas, all noted below. <a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">The examples folder&#39;s own README</a> covers the general mechanics of importing a workflow file, for the cases where you do have one.</p><p>This page assumes you&#39;ve read <a href="./nodes">Nodes Reference</a> and <a href="./expressions">Expressions</a>, and it doesn&#39;t repeat their field-by-field detail. If an example uses a scheduled or webhook trigger, <a href="./background-runs">Background Runs</a> covers what happens once it&#39;s running unattended. If it needs an API key, <a href="./credentials">Credentials</a> covers where that goes.</p><h2 id="getting-started-fetch-and-show" tabindex="-1">Getting Started: Fetch and Show <a class="header-anchor" href="#getting-started-fetch-and-show" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Getting Started: Fetch and Show&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The simplest workflow of the seven: a <strong>Schedule</strong> trigger fires every 10 seconds, an <strong>HTTP Request</strong> node calls a public API, and two <strong>Output</strong> nodes display the result. A good first build, just to confirm a fresh install works before trying anything real.</p><p>This one ships differently from the rest, as a self-hosted Docker deployment package rather than a Markdown walkthrough. <a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">See the Examples README</a> for how to unzip and run it, and to recreate the workflow itself on the canvas.</p><h2 id="scheduled-report" tabindex="-1">Scheduled Report <a class="header-anchor" href="#scheduled-report" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scheduled Report&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Fetches data from an API every weekday morning, extracts one field from the response, and emails the result. Uses a <strong>Schedule</strong> trigger (cron), <strong>HTTP Request</strong>, <strong>Transform Data</strong>, and <strong>Send Email</strong>.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/Scheduled_Report___HTTP___Transform___Email.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full walkthrough</a>.</p><h2 id="webhook-receiver" tabindex="-1">Webhook Receiver <a class="header-anchor" href="#webhook-receiver" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Webhook Receiver&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Accepts an incoming GitHub webhook, checks whether it&#39;s a push to <code>main</code>, and posts to Slack only when it matches. Uses a <strong>Webhook</strong> trigger, <strong>If / Condition</strong>, <strong>Slack</strong>, and <strong>Stop</strong> for everything else.</p><p>Includes a note on why the Webhook node&#39;s Secret field alone doesn&#39;t verify GitHub&#39;s own signature, and what to add if that matters for your setup.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/Webhook_Receiver___GitHub___Slack_Notify.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full walkthrough</a>.</p><h2 id="ai-pipeline" tabindex="-1">AI Pipeline <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-pipeline" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI Pipeline&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Runs once a day: fetches the current top Hacker News story, summarizes it with an AI model, and creates a page in Notion with the result. Uses a <strong>Schedule</strong> trigger (cron), two chained <strong>HTTP Request</strong> nodes, <strong>AI Prompt</strong>, and <strong>Notion</strong>.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/AI_Pipeline___Schedule___Prompt___Notion.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full walkthrough</a>.</p><h2 id="chatbot-with-ai-memory-and-widget" tabindex="-1">Chatbot with AI Memory and Widget <a class="header-anchor" href="#chatbot-with-ai-memory-and-widget" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chatbot with AI Memory and Widget&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A persistent chatbot: each message from an embedded chat widget gets a reply from an AI model, and the exchange is saved so the next message continues the same conversation instead of starting over. Uses <strong>AI Memory</strong> to read history before the model runs and write it back after, with an <strong>AI Prompt</strong> node in between.</p><p>One of the longer walkthroughs. Covers embedding the widget script on a web page, plus security notes on token exposure and prompt injection worth reading before you expose one publicly.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/Chatbot___AI_Memory___Widget.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full walkthrough</a>.</p><h2 id="ai-agent-router" tabindex="-1">AI Agent Router <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-agent-router" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI Agent Router&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An <strong>AI Agent</strong> asks for one of two tools, the workflow checks the request and runs the tool, and a second AI Agent answers from the result. Uses <strong>AI Agent</strong> twice, <strong>If / Condition</strong>, <strong>Switch</strong>, <strong>HTTP Request</strong>, <strong>Set Variable</strong>, <strong>Merge</strong> and <strong>Output</strong>. It shows how to act on an agent&#39;s tool requests with a fixed, visible number of model calls, and what to check before anything runs.</p><p>Ships as a workflow file as well as a walkthrough: drag <a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/AI_Agent_Router.aerini" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>AI_Agent_Router.aerini</code></a> onto the canvas, then pick an API key for each AI Agent. The walkthrough also covers the safety rules for wiring a model&#39;s tool arguments into other nodes.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/AI_Agent_Router.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full walkthrough</a>.</p><h2 id="ai-agent-loop" tabindex="-1">AI Agent Loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#ai-agent-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;AI Agent Loop&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An <strong>AI Agent</strong> runs inside a <strong>Loop</strong>: each turn it answers or asks for one of two tools, the workflow checks the request and runs the tool, and the next turn continues from the result. Uses <strong>Loop</strong>, <strong>AI Agent</strong>, <strong>If / Condition</strong>, <strong>Switch</strong>, <strong>HTTP Request</strong>, <strong>Set Variable</strong>, <strong>Merge</strong> and <strong>Output</strong>. The number of turns is the length of a list you set, and <strong>Stop When</strong> ends the loop as soon as the agent answers or a request is rejected. There is no token budget or time cap, which the walkthrough says plainly.</p><p>Ships as a workflow file as well as a walkthrough: drag <a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/AI_Agent_Loop.aerini" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>AI_Agent_Loop.aerini</code></a> onto the canvas, then pick an API key for the AI Agent. The pattern was tested on Gemini; other providers are untested in a Loop. The walkthrough also covers the safety rules for tools that run in a loop.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Panchak2d/Aerini/blob/main/examples/AI_Agent_Loop.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full walkthrough</a>.</p><h2 id="see-also" tabindex="-1">See also <a class="header-anchor" href="#see-also" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;See also&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./nodes">Nodes Reference</a>, for every field these examples reference</li><li><a href="./expressions">Expressions</a>, for the <code>{{...}}</code> syntax wiring node outputs together</li><li><a href="./credentials">Credentials</a>, for the API keys most of these need</li><li><a href="./background-runs">Background Runs</a>, for what happens once a Schedule or Webhook example is running unattended</li></ul>',30)])])}const u=t(n,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
